{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790428584,"nonce":"ksilsEnuIvivoJ3p/hGL82hwwek=","title":"Zyntax languages on AOT and wasm: compiled and linked, no interpreter","message":"AOT and wasm targets embed no interpreter (direction 2026-09-26, see\n0683393 and f98f9f0). Lua, Python and ZynML modules therefore ship on\nthose targets only as compiled code, linked beside Ash's and WrenLift's.\n\nNeeded:\n- Zyntax emits object files (native) and wasm modules (wasm) for a\n  module's HIR, referencing the core's symbols: one heap, one scheduler.\n- Exported members follow caribou::link's symbol and signature rule\n  (docs/architecture/linking.md), so the other languages' AOT output\n  links to them, and theirs to it. The published set seeds reachability.\n- The custom wasm linker takes Zyntax's modules as well as Ash's and\n  WrenLift's.\n- .zrtl plugins link statically on these targets.\n- What Zyntax does today only at run time must have a compiled form, or\n  be refused at build with a message naming it. That covers\n  dynamically typed paths that need the tiered JIT, and grammar\n  snapshots.\n\nOpen question: which of Zyntax's backends (Cranelift, LLVM) emit\nrelocatable objects and wasm today. Ask the zyntax session before\nplanning further.","files":null}]}